UID:af40d5bdcb670415@role-healthcare-human-services.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260826T200359Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260826
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] US Department of Labor cites Pennsylvania bri
 ck manufacturer for willfully exposing workers to silica hazards\, propose
 s $496K in fines
DESCRIPTION:WATSONTOWN\, PA – The U.S. Department of Labor has cited a 
 brick manufacturer with three willful and four serious violations for know
 ingly exposing its workers to unsafe levels of respirable silica dust at i
 ts Watsontown plant.On Feb. 12\, 2026\, investigators with the department
 ’s Occupational Safety and Health Administration inspected General Shale
  Brick Inc. – doing business as Watsontown Brick Company – as part of 
 the agency’s Site‑Specific Targeting inspection plan and national emph
 asis program on silica as well as a review of the company’s injury and i
 llness records. OSHA found that workers in Plant 3\, where molded brick is
  made\, breathed in silica dust above safe limits. The company knew about 
 the exposure but failed to fix the problem or require workers to wear resp
 irators.OSHA also determined the company failed to test workers’ respira
 tor fit each year\, did not establish regulated areas where employees are 
 exposed to silica\, did not check silica exposure levels for all silica-in
 volved jobs\, and did not provide required medical exams for workers expos
 ed to silica for more than 30 days a year. A past violation for missing re
 spirator fit tests\, at the company’s Augusta\, Georgia site\, led to an
  added repeat violation.OSHA issued three willful\, four serious\, one rep
 eat\, and one other-than-serious violation\, with proposed penalties of $4
 96\,528.The company has 15 business days from receipt of its citations to 
 comply\, request an informal conference with OSHA’s area director\, or c
 ontest the findings before the independent Occupational Safety and Health 
 Review Commission. Check the OSHA establishment search page periodically
  for any changes in the inspection or penalty status.Learn more about prot
 ecting workers from silica hazards. Employers can also contact the agency 
 for free help meeting OSHA safety standards.\nhttp://www.dol.gov/newsroom/
 releases/osha/osha20260826
URL:http://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/osha/osha20260826
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:11fc72c04ae7d6ea@role-healthcare-human-services.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260826T200359Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260826
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Trump Administration issues guidance on healt
 h-contingent wellness programs\, including tobacco cessation programs
DESCRIPTION:WASHINGTON – The U.S. Department of Labor announced today th
 at its Employee Benefits Security Administration\, along with the departm
 ents of Health and Human Services and Treasury\, issued guidance related 
 to questions raised in dozens of class-action lawsuits challenging the tob
 acco surcharges some employers add to premiums for their group health plan
 s through workplace wellness programs. The Health Insurance Portability 
 and Accountability Act of 1996 and the Affordable Care Act allow employer
 s to reward employees for healthy habits through wellness programs. Wellne
 ss programs can cover things like nutrition\, weight loss\, tobacco cessat
 ion\, fitness\, alcohol and drug abuse assistance\, managing stress\, or g
 eneral health education.The FAQs released today announce the departments
 ’ approach to enforcement for health-contingent wellness programs\, whi
 ch are programs that reward an employee if they satisfy a standard related
  to a health factor. Until further guidance or regulations are issued\, th
 e departments will not take enforcement action against plans or issuers th
 at do not give employees the wellness program reward retroactively to the 
 beginning of the plan year after the employee completes a reasonable alter
 native standard. Under the terms of the enforcement relief in these FAQs\,
  plans and issuers only need to provide the reward prospectively\, from th
 e point where the employee completes the alternative standard\, if retroac
 tive rewards are not otherwise provided.The FAQs also address plans’ and
  issuers’ disclosure obligations related to health-contingent wellness p
 rograms. Specifically\, if plan materials merely mention that a health-con
 tingent wellness program is available\, without describing its terms\, the
  plan or issuer is not required to disclose the availability of a reasonab
 le alternative standard to qualify for the reward under the wellness progr
 am.“The experimental nature of wellness programs enables them to drive s
 ignificant and clinically meaningful health main\nhttp://www.dol.gov/newsr
 oom/releases/ebsa/ebsa20260826
URL:http://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/ebsa/ebsa20260826
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:48183e296f0fb7a0@role-healthcare-human-services.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260826T200223Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260826
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Safe Homes Awareness Month - September
DESCRIPTION:WHEREAS\n\, the 130th Maine Legislature unanimously passed L.D
 . 1861\, An Act to Establish the Safe Homes Program\, and as Governor\, I 
 signed the bill into law on April 7\, 2022\; and\nWHEREAS\n\, this biparti
 san law promotes the safe storage of prescription drugs\, firearms and oth
 er dangerous weapons in homes or public spaces\; and\nWHEREAS\n\, the Safe
  Homes Program is an optional\, voluntary grant program that builds on the
  work of L.D. 1140\, An Act To Establish a Sales Tax Exemption for the Pur
 chase of Firearm Safety Devices\, by making storage devices\, such as gun 
 safes\, more affordable for people who want to buy them\; and\nWHEREAS\n\,
  the Safe Homes Program Fund will provide grants for purchasing safety dev
 ices for the safe storage of prescription drugs\, firearms\, and dangerous
  weapons in homes or public spaces\; and\nWHEREAS\n\, according to a repor
 t by the U.S. CDC\, there were 181 deaths by firearms in the State of Main
 e in 2024\, with over 86 percent of the deaths the result of suicide\; and
 \nWHEREAS\n\, in 2025\, there were 390 confirmed and suspected fatal drug 
 overdoses\, 20.4 percent lower than the total confirmed fatal overdoses fo
 r 2024\; and\nWHEREAS\n\, by ensuring that firearms and prescription drugs
  are properly stored and secured\, this law will reduce accidental gun dea
 ths\, prevent suicide\, and improve public health\; and\nWHEREAS\n\, in ac
 cordance with 1 MRSA § 150-R\, the month of September of each year is des
 ignated as Safe Homes Awareness Month to raise awareness of the importance
  of safety in the home and the assistance that is available through the Sa
 fe Homes Program established in Title 25\, section 7001\;\nNOW\, THEREFORE
 \n\, be it resolved that I\, Janet T. Mills\, Governor of the State of Mai
 ne\, do hereby proclaim September 2026 as\nSafe Homes Awareness Month\nthr
 oughout the State of Maine\, and I urge all citizens to recognize this obs
 ervance\, promote the proper storage of prescription drugs and firearms\, 
 and learn more about the assistance available through the Safe Homes Progr
 am.\nI\nhttp://www.maine.gov/governor/mills/official_documents/proclamatio
 ns/2026-08-safe-homes-awareness-month-september
URL:http://www.maine.gov/governor/mills/official_documents/proclamations/2
 026-08-safe-homes-awareness-month-september
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:e2d0fa041a7a9f8e@role-healthcare-human-services.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260826T200223Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260826
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Library Card Sign-up Month - September
DESCRIPTION:WHEREAS\n\, libraries across the State of Maine are essential 
 to education\, imagination\, and opportunity\, providing early literacy pr
 ograms\, digital learning tools\, and lifelong pathways to knowledge and g
 rowth\; and\nWHEREAS\n\, Maine's libraries welcome all people\, serving as
  inclusive and accessible community spaces that connect individuals across
  cultures\, generations\, and life experiences\, and foster a strong sense
  of belonging\; and\nWHEREAS\n\, Maine's 257 public libraries reflect and 
 honor the diversity of Maine people\, honoring Wabanaki voices\, supportin
 g new Mainers\, preserving Franco-American heritage\, and uplifting rural\
 , coastal\, and urban communities alike through collections\, services and
  programs that are locally rooted\; and\nWHEREAS\n\, our libraries help Ma
 ine people navigate life's challenges by offering trusted information\, hi
 gh-speed internet\, job search and career support\, and vital connections 
 to health\, housing\, and social services – especially in communities wh
 ere other resources are limited or unavailable\; and\nWHEREAS\n\, our libr
 aries contribute to Maine's economic resilience by equipping workers\, stu
 dents\, entrepreneurs\, and small business owners with free access to trai
 ning\, online resources\, technology\, and other tools to succeed in this 
 changing world\; and\nWHEREAS\n\, through good times and bad\, Maine's lib
 rary staff have remained steadfast in their service – innovating\, adapt
 ing\, and going above and beyond to meet the evolving needs of their commu
 nities\; and\nWHEREAS\n\, a library card opens doors for every Mainer – 
 to books and broadband\, to curiosity and creativity\, to ideas and inspir
 ation\, to lifelong learning and opportunity\, to the local and global com
 munity\;\nNOW\, THEREFORE\n\, be it resolved that I\, Janet T. Mills\, Gov
 ernor of the State of Maine\, do hereby proclaim September 2026 as\nLibrar
 y Card Sign-up Month\nthroughout the State of Maine\, and I encourage all 
 Mainers to sign up for a library card and explore the many resources\, pro
 grams\, and opportu\nhttp://www.maine.gov/governor/mills/official_document
 s/proclamations/2026-08-library-card-sign-month-september
URL:http://www.maine.gov/governor/mills/official_documents/proclamations/2
 026-08-library-card-sign-month-september
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
